There's been a recent short thread (Subject: NeXT,Ibm,vax nethack help...) in
which a couple of people took exception to what I'd written about nethack
ports.  I'd written:

>Perhaps the FAQ needs clarification....Folks, please
>let me know if you think I've got this correct, and if the FAQ should
>have some clarification.  Thanks!

And then I excerpted from an E-mail I'd sent to vittallo@ssd.com.mot.com who'd
asked about various ports of NetHack:

>... The only "official" ports of Nethack are for:

>     MS-DOS
>     OS/2
>     Macintosh
>     Atari
>     Amiga
>
>There are no other officially ported versions.  Other systems (VMS, Unix,
>etc.) must port their own.  So, there's no official SUN or SPARC version.  The
>FAQ specifically mentions the binary versions for the personal computer
>systems mentioned above, and how to obtain any of them, or the source code to
>do your own port.  Somewhere, in the linc.cis.upenn.edu files, I thought I
>once saw a list of computer systems that were known to have had ports done
>successfully, but no official "ports" except the ones above exist.  As I'm not
>a member of the NetHack officialdom, just a FAQs keeper, I have no knowledge
>about why things were set up the way they were.  I can assume, though, that
>ports of NetHack to Unix based systems are simple enough and the various Unix
>platforms varied enought that its easier to support and manage in the form
>that its in.

Then, Pat Rankin (rankin@eql.caltech.edu) wrote:

>     I think you've got it wrong.  ;-) ...
>     Perhaps you've got an unusal conception of the meaning of "port".

That's it.  We've got some basic terminology differences.

My understanding of a "ported" product:  binary and supported.
 
This comes from my understanding of the commercial Open Systems
marketplace, where 3rd party software vendors "port" their products
to various Unix platforms.  I'm certainly willing to adjust the this
definition to meet the needs of the FAQ.

>The Unix version is what all the other ports are based on.  The VMS port
>is as "official" as any of the others.  Binaries are provided for the
>microcomputer versions because of the number of people who wouldn't be
>able to build them otherwise.  People on multi-user systems are expected
>to build from sources themselves.  VMS binaries happen to be available
>at an alternate site.

I assumed 1) that a "port" would be pre-generated, and 2) if it isn't stored at
linc.cis.upenn.edu, it isn't official.

>     If you look at the 'README' file included with the sources, it has a
>list of systems where NetHack has been demonstrated or at least reported
>to build and run.  The list of Unix variants is vary large...

Unless these variants are all available for distribution and supported,
I can't see calling them "official".

Then, jwalz@rsg1.er.usgs.gov sent me e-mail and said:

>...there's a pseudo-official VMS binary from a devteam member
>along with VMS-packaged sources on a DECUS tape and uars.acd.ucar.edu.)

Pat mentioned this one, also.  How come it isn't official?

>Sun distributes hack 1.0.2 (from 1985!) as part of its optional games on
>its OS tapes, which I suppose makes it official Sun if not official NetHack.

Hack isn't NetHack, and while the older game is still of interest to a number
of people, it is beyond the scope of my FAQ sheet.

>I would say that anything supported in the source (accessible by just setting
>the right defines) is an existing port.  Generally there is an installation
>file (Install.xxx) for each major supported OS variant, as there certainly
>is for VMS and UNIX.

This is probably the best way to determine what's "official" or not.  As there
is a VMS source variant, then there is official source but no official binary
for VMS at the moment, according to this better definition.

I also got a letter from Jared J. Brennan (brennan@cs.jhu.edu) that discussed
these same issues.

Would everyone be happy if I updated the FAQ to mention the official source
variants, the official binary variants, and the one unofficial VMS binary?

Thanks for all your comments; I appreciate your assitance with getting
the FAQ straight.

